// Editorial explanations adapted from the user's supplied MT0–15 research.
// References are resolved by build-military.cjs; no private save data is used.
module.exports=[
{
tier:0,title:'Build your first working tower',phase:'Early game',
goal:'Develop a dependable combat blueprint, unlock more regions, and earn your first Headquarters promotion.',
terms:[['Military Tier (MT)','Your overall prestige stage. It determines which systems you can access. It is separate from the strength of an individual module.'],['Module and blueprint','A module supplies an attack, defense or utility effect. Your blueprint is the collection you equip for a tower test. Owning a module does not automatically equip it.'],['Module level and tier','Levels improve a module within its current tier. A tier is a larger upgrade stage. Workshop upgrades persist between ordinary tower tests; upgrades bought during a run are temporary.']],
targets:['Open Headquarters and read the objectives for MT1. The supplied older reference lists 10 maximized modules and 3 regions; check the current screen before treating either as a fixed target.','Have a blueprint that clears the next required region reliably, and maximize the number of modules Headquarters requests.'],
steps:[['Build around the starting modules','Use Basic Attack, Attack Speed, Stone Foundation and Basic Regeneration as a simple starting point. Attack determines whether enemies die before surrounding you; foundation and regeneration give you time to recover. Upgrade the Workshop through the Construction Firm when building limits stop further progress.',['S03']],['Spend on the failure you can see','If enemies accumulate while you stay healthy, improve damage and attack speed. If the tower loses health despite killing enemies, improve its foundation and healing. Make a few upgrades, repeat the same test, and compare the result before changing several things at once. This is a diagnostic approach rather than a required build order.',['S03']],['Collect useful Forest rewards','The community references lists Basic Bouncing at wave 10, Multishot at 30, Nature’s Touch at 40 and Wave Resources at 50. Check each reward in the module reference. Bouncing and multishot help one attack deal with more targets; Nature’s Touch combines damage with healing. Equip useful rewards and upgrade them before testing again.',['S03','S06']],['Use Easy clears to open the map','Clear available Easy regions to unlock subsequent regions. Return to a reliable farming run when the next clear is too difficult. A repeatable run that funds upgrades is useful even if it does not set a record. Once combat works, spend on the additional modules needed for the promotion objective.',['S03','S02']],['Promote with the reset in mind','Read the reset preview in Headquarters before confirming. Promotion changes your module levels and cube-resource economy, while unlocking new progression tools. Do not assume a military promotion behaves like simply ending a tower test. After reaching MT1, inspect workers and your first military perk.',['S02','S05']]],
trouble:[['I unlocked a module but nothing changed','Check that it is equipped in the blueprint you actually started with. Then check its Workshop level.'],['I can clear regions but cannot promote','A region clear and a module objective are separate. Read every unfinished Headquarters objective rather than repeating a completed combat goal.']],
check:['Every MT1 objective is complete in Headquarters.','You have read the reset preview and know which upgrades you will rebuild.'],
next:'At MT1, use workers and improved production to make the next rebuild easier. Backups is a useful first-perk candidate: its protection helps on later promotions, not retroactively on the promotion you just completed.',sources:['S02','S03','S05','S06']
},
{
tier:1,title:'Build an economy that supports your tower',phase:'Early game',
goal:'Make Workshop upgrades affordable while collecting enough modules and regions for MT2.',
terms:[['Workers','Assignments that repeat supported tasks. Read each assignment’s requirements and output so that it helps your current bottleneck.'],['Factory producer','A crafted source of recurring resources. Building producers turns part of your progression into steady income rather than relying exclusively on combat rewards.'],['Backups','A military perk intended to preserve a baseline of module progress through later promotions. Check its current wording before choosing it.']],
targets:['Check the MT2 module and region objectives in Headquarters. The supplied older table lists 25 maximized modules and 5 regions; the in-game counters take priority.','Maintain a reliable farming blueprint while expanding permanent production.'],
steps:[['Rebuild the useful part of your Workshop','First restore the damage and survival modules that made the previous tier work. You need a dependable source of income before spreading purchases across your entire collection. Test on a familiar region so you can tell when the tower is ready to move forward.',['S03','S04']],['Raise building limits when they block production','Use the Construction Firm to improve the buildings needed for your next step. A building upgrade is valuable when it opens a recipe, capacity or upgrade you can use; it is not automatically the best purchase simply because it is available.',['S04']],['Start a small Factory production chain','Choose an accessible producer recipe. Inspect its ingredients, make the required machines, and work backward to the ores and intermediate materials you need. If one ingredient is always missing, improve that stage first. Keeping every machine busy matters less than supplying the ingredient that is holding up the next producer.',['S04']],['Support the Factory with the Mine','Develop mining and refining together so that higher-tier ingredients become possible. Avoid spending all available materials on a future recipe while your present production remains unable to replace them. Assign workers to supported repetitive tasks that you otherwise keep returning to manually.',['S04','S05']],['Expand regions and finish the module counter','Alternate combat pushes with collecting missing modules. When the tower is strong enough for the required regions, direct more resources toward maximizing the extra modules Headquarters requests. A module does not have to remain in your combat blueprint to contribute to a collection objective.',['S02','S06']]],
trouble:[['Enemies are easy, but upgrades take forever','Treat this as an income problem. Compare the time needed for another producer or production upgrade with the time spent repeating tower tests.'],['The Factory feels complicated','Pick one concrete item to craft next. Follow only that recipe’s chain until it is complete, then choose the next bottleneck.']],
check:['Headquarters shows the MT2 module and region objectives complete.','Your income can rebuild your core combat modules after promotion.'],next:'MT2 is a good point to broaden your sources of modules. Challenges and Laboratory experiments can fill gaps that ordinary region farming does not.',sources:['S02','S03','S04','S05','S06']
},
{
tier:2,title:'Expand your module collection',phase:'Early game',
goal:'Use regions, challenges and experiments to prepare for MT3 without spending everything on one expensive attack.',
terms:[['Challenge','A tower test with additional restrictions or rules. Read them before choosing a blueprint: a normal farming setup may not satisfy them.'],['Laboratory experiment','An element-themed progression system with its own tasks and rewards. It provides another route to modules.'],['Software','Headquarters research that changes how tower testing progresses. Unlocking software and researching it are separate steps.']],
targets:['Complete the MT3 objectives shown in Headquarters. The older reference lists 40 maximized modules and 7 regions.','Keep your economy growing while you collect modules from more than one system.'],
steps:[['Open up the buildings now available to you','Inspect new Construction Firm options, including the Statue of Cubos and Shipyard as they become available. Learn what each produces before committing heavily. Continue to support the Workshop and Factory; new buildings should help progression rather than consume every resource at once.',['S04']],['Try manageable challenges deliberately','Read the challenge’s equipment restrictions, enemy rules and victory condition. Assemble a blueprint around those constraints. If a challenge remains far beyond your current strength, leave it and gain modules elsewhere instead of treating it as mandatory immediately.',['S06']],['Work on Laboratory rewards','Choose an experiment whose next task you understand and can complete with your current resources. Check the module reference for the reward and its conditions. Alternate Laboratory work with regional drops so you are not waiting on one rare source for every missing module.',['S06','S04']],['Keep Headquarters research supplied','Improve available servers and use them for software you can currently research. Support the Power Plant where electricity is restricting progress. You are preparing an infrastructure advantage, not merely increasing tower damage.',['S07']],['Finish your objectives and learn Critical Wavejump','After reaching MT3, inspect and research Critical Wavejump when available. It uses your previous wave record to help revisit familiar territory. Establishing a highscore still requires a tower that can survive and kill at that point; a shortcut back to a record cannot fix a weak blueprint.',['S02','S07']]],
trouble:[['My blueprint is full of attacks but performs poorly','Adding more effects is not always better than investing in a compatible core. Keep enough defense to survive and focus upgrades on attacks doing useful work.'],['A challenge rejects or defeats my normal build','Re-read its restrictions and reward conditions. Treat it as a separate puzzle rather than an ordinary region clear.']],
check:['The MT3 counters in Headquarters are complete.','You know which available software still needs research.'],next:'MT3 leads to Facility AI. Keep improving the economy now so automation has useful production to operate once you unlock it.',sources:['S02','S04','S06','S07']
},
{
tier:3,title:'Prepare for Facility AI',phase:'Early game',
goal:'Complete the last pre-AI promotion and identify the repetitive tasks you want to automate at MT4.',
terms:[['Facility AI','A scripting system for performing supported game actions under specified conditions. It is more flexible than a fixed worker assignment.'],['Specialization','A military choice that opens a particular building’s additional development route. Different choices favor different play styles.']],
targets:['Complete the MT4 module and region requirements. The supplied older table lists 60 maximized modules and 9 regions.','Prepare a dependable production loop and combat blueprint before attempting to automate either.'],
steps:[['Improve the blueprint you already understand','Push more regions with your established attack and survival core. When progress stops, identify whether the problem is damage, survival or time spent farming resources. Buy upgrades that address that specific limit.',['S04','S06']],['Use several routes to missing modules','Consult the module list and mix regional farming with Laboratory, Mine-related rewards and manageable challenges. A difficult random drop is not necessarily the cheapest way to gain one more module toward promotion.',['S06']],['Write down your repetitive actions','Notice tasks such as refining an ingredient, crafting an intermediate item or buying in-run module upgrades. Choose one task with a clear starting condition and stopping condition. This gives you a small, understandable first automation instead of a large script whose effects are hard to diagnose.',['S07']],['Promote, then set up Facility AI','Unlocking MT4 gives access to Facility AI; inspect its controls and relevant research in Headquarters. Test a small script while watching it. Confirm that it acts on the intended building, has its required inputs, and stops or waits appropriately when those inputs run out. Building-dependent actions may require the relevant interface to be open. Do not assume scripts run offline.',['S02','S07']],['Choose a specialization for your actual goal','The supplied community advice favors Power Plant/Dyson Sphere for tower-testing boosts, with Mine/Asteroid Mining as an alternative for varied rewards. Factory expansion can be valuable but demanding to develop early. Compare the current perk descriptions and costs; this is a recommendation, not a universal best choice.',['S04','S05']]],
trouble:[['My first script does nothing','Check that its required feature is researched, the correct task is selected, the needed inputs exist, and the relevant building is open where required. Test one action before a full loop.'],['Automation produces the wrong material','Stop it and simplify the task. Verify quantities and conditions manually before allowing it to repeat.']],
check:['Headquarters shows MT4 ready.','You have a short list of useful first automation tasks.'],next:'At MT4, the next wall is collecting and maximizing many modules. Use automation to support that workload, then aim for Wave Momentum at MT5.',sources:['S02','S04','S05','S06','S07']
},
{
tier:4,title:'Finish the module grind for Wave Momentum',phase:'Early game',
goal:'Collect and maximize enough modules to reach MT5 while your production handles the cost.',
terms:[['Random module drop','A reward with a chance to appear after a run. Repeating an eligible run provides repeated opportunities; reaching one very high wave does not guarantee it.'],['Wave Momentum','Software unlocked with MT5 that makes enemy kills contribute to faster wave advancement. You cannot rely on it before that promotion.']],
targets:['The community references cites the v1.0.1 changelog for reducing the MT5 objective from 100 to 80 maximized modules. Check Headquarters for the full current objective list.','Complete the region requirement and fund the Workshop purchases without dismantling your working economy.'],
steps:[['Make a missing-module shortlist','Open the Workshop collection and compare missing modules with the unlock reference. Separate random region drops from Laboratory and challenge rewards. This avoids repeatedly farming a region for a reward it cannot provide.',['S06']],['Use short, repeatable runs for random drops','Before Wave Momentum, the supplied community strategy suggests roughly waves 30–50 at a difficulty you can repeat quickly. Treat this as a starting point, not a fixed optimum. Compare successful runs per minute and survivability; a harder difficulty is not useful if it makes every run much slower or unreliable.',['S06','S13']],['Let automation support production','Use small, verified Factory or upgrade routines to reduce repetitive work. Keep ingredients and resource conversions supplied. Avoid leaving a script running simply because it runs: check that its output helps fund the next Workshop purchases.',['S07','S04']],['Spend toward the promotion counter','After your farming build works, maximize additional affordable modules until Headquarters registers the requirement. Check the in-game meaning of maximized for your available module tier. Increasing a few favorite modules while leaving the rest unfinished may not move the counter you need.',['S01','S02']],['Research the new software after promoting','At MT5, prioritize Wave Momentum research. Inspect Duplicates when choosing a military perk: the community references recommends its protection for later promotions. Read when that protection begins rather than expecting it to restore a reset that already occurred.',['S05','S07']]],
trouble:[['A guide says I need 100 modules','That is an older requirement flagged by the community references. Use the current Headquarters objective and the developer changelog.'],['I own enough modules but the objective is incomplete','Unlocked and maximized are different states. Check which modules have reached the required upgrade endpoint.']],
check:['Headquarters confirms the maximized-module and region objectives.','You are ready to research Wave Momentum after reaching MT5.'],next:'The next objective shifts toward total module tier. Broad, affordable tier upgrades become increasingly important.',sources:['S01','S02','S04','S05','S06','S07','S13']
},
{
tier:5,title:'Understand total module tier',phase:'Endless progression',
goal:'Expand your collection and upgrade module tiers across it to reach MT6.',
terms:[['Total module tier','The sum of the tiers of your modules. A tier-5 module contributes five; buying more levels inside that tier does not add to the sum.'],['Wave Storm','Software available at MT6 that links normal-mode completion to wave-acceleration benefits. It is a reward to prepare for, not a tool already required at MT5.']],
targets:['Use Headquarters for the current unlocked-module and total-tier thresholds; the community references explicitly says the old total-tier numbers changed in v1.0.','Unlock the remaining regions. Do not rely on the obsolete Boss 2 promotion requirement reported in older tables.'],
steps:[['Read the right counter','Separate unlocked modules, module levels and total tiers. For example, raising twenty modules from tier 5 to tier 6 adds twenty to the total. Raising those modules’ levels without tiering them adds nothing to this objective. Use the Headquarters counter to confirm the effect of your purchases.',['S02']],['Finish opening the map','Complete the necessary Easy clears to unlock the remaining regions, then collect useful missing rewards. You are building both a broader collection and access to more farming options.',['S02','S06']],['Buy affordable tier increases broadly','Compare the cost of one additional tier on several modules. If your combat build already works, purchasing multiple inexpensive tiers can move the promotion counter farther than one very costly upgrade. Keep enough strength in your actual blueprint to continue farming.',['S02','S06']],['Scale the resource supply','Keep Factory producers and useful Trading Post bonuses developing. When production is the limiting factor, improve it before spending long periods manually repeating low-yield tower tests. Use Facility AI for understood repetitive tasks rather than automating an inefficient loop unchanged.',['S04','S07']],['Promote and inspect Town Perks','After reaching MT6, choose Town Perk investments around your current shortage: resource income, combat or production speed. Research Wave Storm, then begin completing more normal region/difficulty combinations to support it.',['S02','S07']]],
trouble:[['My total tier barely changes after many upgrades','You may be buying levels rather than tiers. Watch the total-tier counter after a single known tier upgrade.'],['An old guide insists on a boss kill','The community references flags removal of the MT6 boss requirement. Follow the actual Headquarters checklist, not a historical table.']],
check:['The current total-tier and collection requirements are complete.','All required regions are unlocked.'],next:'MT6 asks you to turn enemy kills and software into sustained Endless speed. The next documented wave milestone is one million.',sources:['S01','S02','S04','S06','S07']
},
{
tier:6,title:'Push Endless to one million waves',phase:'Endless progression',
goal:'Build a fast, sustainable Endless run and reach the documented wave milestone for MT7.',
terms:[['Endless mode','A test intended to continue past a normal clear. It is where large wave-record objectives become practical.'],['Normal completion','Finishing a region at a particular normal difficulty. It is distinct from merely unlocking the region or setting an Endless record.'],['Wave advancement','How quickly the wave counter moves. When enemies already die immediately, more damage may barely improve this.']],
targets:['Work toward wave 1,000,000, the milestone described in the supplied tier research. Confirm it and all additional requirements in Headquarters.','Use the current total-module-tier threshold; do not copy the outdated total from older tables.'],
steps:[['Complete available wave-software research','Inspect Headquarters for unlocked but unresearched software and improve servers where research speed is holding you back. Make sure relevant software is enabled during the intended run. Ownership alone is not enough.',['S07']],['Increase normal-mode coverage','Clear additional region/difficulty combinations that your tower can handle. These completions support Wave Storm. Choose manageable clears before spending a long time forcing one difficult combination.',['S07','S12']],['Build for rapid, reliable kills','Use a coherent damage source with attack speed and useful multi-target effects such as Multishot, bouncing or splash. Maintain enough healing and defense that the run survives unattended stretches. An impressive burst of kills followed by a quick death is not sustained progression.',['S06','S12']],['Run Endless and diagnose the limiting factor','Use Wave Momentum and appropriate researched acceleration software. Watch whether the tower is struggling to kill, struggling to survive, or simply advancing waves slowly. Those are three different problems and should lead to different purchases.',['S07']],['Finish the milestone and remaining objectives','Once the wave requirement is registered, return to any unfinished module collection or tier objectives. At MT7, inspect the newly available software before attempting the much larger next record.',['S02','S07']]],
trouble:[['Enemies vanish but the wave number crawls','Check software research, enabled states and normal-mode completion. Raw attack upgrades are unlikely to be the first solution.'],['The run is fast but repeatedly dies','Add enough survival to keep the acceleration running. A slightly slower stable build can outperform repeated failed pushes.']],
check:['Headquarters has registered the wave objective.','The collection and current total-tier objectives are complete.'],next:'MT7 adds more acceleration tools. Their interaction matters far more on the route to wave 100 billion than on your early regional clears.',sources:['S02','S06','S07','S12']
},
{
tier:7,title:'Turn wave acceleration into Era access',phase:'Endless progression',
goal:'Reach wave 100 billion and unlock MT8’s Era tools.',
terms:[['Wave Persistence','Helps retain accumulated Wave Momentum progress, according to the software reference.'],['Wave Catalyst','Uses Critical Wavejump activity to support acceleration. Its value depends on having a useful prior record.'],['Era','The next scale of tower testing, reached at the 100-billion-wave boundary. Its combat systems are explained in the MT8 guide; you do not need to master them for this push.']],
targets:['Work toward wave 100,000,000,000 and confirm the promotion conditions in Headquarters.','Complete the current module collection and total-tier objectives alongside the wave push.'],
steps:[['Research the MT7 acceleration tools','Inspect Wave Persistence, Instability, Vortex, Catalyst and Endurance. Read each current tooltip and enable the tools that support your intended run. The community references describes Persistence as supporting Momentum retention, Instability as improving Wavejump, and Vortex/Endurance as improving streaming speed.',['S07']],['Build a wider base of normal clears','Work through more of the 15 regions and six normal difficulties, up to 90 combinations where practical. This is useful support for Wave Storm, not a separate claim that all 90 are mandatory for promotion.',['S07','S12']],['Establish a meaningful Endless record','Critical Wavejump is more useful when there is a substantial record to jump toward. Use your reliable combat build to push, then compare how much faster you revisit that territory on a later run. Do not restart constantly without understanding what progress your software retains.',['S07']],['Keep kills and survival steady','Continue improving a high-kill-rate blueprint and permanent bonuses from your available buildings. When enemies already die quickly, focus on acceleration and uptime instead of repeatedly buying damage that does not change the run.',['S12','S06']],['Let the acceleration develop','Allow a stable run time to gain the benefit of streaming and acceleration software. Older community routes include specific restart timing; treat those as experiments, not guaranteed instructions for your version. Compare actual time to a repeatable milestone.',['S12','S07']]],
trouble:[['I am stuck near my old million-wave record','Check Momentum, Storm and Persistence before buying more attack. Also verify that you are in the intended Endless mode.'],['Following an old timer makes my run worse','Software behavior has changed across versions. Use the current descriptions and your measured progress rather than a fixed historical timer.']],
check:['The 100-billion-wave milestone and remaining Headquarters objectives are complete.','After promotion, inspect the Era Workshop and newly available software.'],next:'At MT8, enemies gain Era mechanics. Ordinary damage upgrades alone will no longer explain whether a blueprint succeeds.',sources:['S02','S07','S08','S12']
},
{
tier:8,title:'Learn Era shields and elemental powers',phase:'Era progression',
goal:'Build a tower that can handle the first Eras and work toward Era 5 for MT9.',
terms:[['Era Shield','An enormous scaling multiplier affecting enemy durability and damage. Ordinary module upgrades alone can struggle to keep up.'],['Era power','An extra ability associated with an enemy element. Learn which power is causing trouble before spending to counter it.'],['Divisor','An upgrade that divides down part of the enemy’s Era scaling. Damage and health-related divisors solve different parts of the problem.']],
targets:['Reach the documented Era 5 milestone and confirm current module objectives in Headquarters.','Learn the difference between permanent Era research and upgrades bought inside one tower test.'],
steps:[['Start with a manageable region','Use an easier region/difficulty such as Forest Easy while learning the system. The purpose is a stable environment where you can see which element and ability stop the run, rather than changing several sources of difficulty at once.',['S08']],['Open the Era Workshop','Use the Workshop’s stairs to inspect the Era systems. Analysis reveals information through combat interactions; research works toward persistent improvements; experiments develop further rewards and reductions. Read unlock conditions as each tab becomes available rather than assuming all three are immediately usable.',['S08']],['Gather analysis deliberately','Run a blueprint that survives long enough to register the needed hits or kills. Read the specific analysis objective: an attack-focused build may not help a task that needs received hits. The community references notes that v1.0 removed the old minimum-damage rule for registering Era hits.',['S08','S01']],['Improve divisors and counter troublesome powers','Buy the relevant available research with the resources the screen requests. Reduce the costs of disabling powers that are blocking your run, then use those disables in testing. The supplied walkthrough suggests beginning with problematic Forest elements such as Earth and Neutral rather than trying to solve every element immediately.',['S08']],['Advance a little, then reassess','Reach a stable Era 1 before pushing farther. If progression fails, identify whether enemies became too durable, too dangerous, or acquired an ability your build cannot handle. Improve the appropriate divisor or counter and retry.',['S08']]],
trouble:[['My strong old blueprint suddenly deals almost no useful damage','Inspect the Era multipliers and powers. This is the point where researching counters can matter more than another ordinary damage upgrade.'],['Analysis is not progressing','Check its exact required element and interaction. Avoid assuming all damage or all kills count toward every analysis.']],
check:['You can explain which divisors and disables keep your run working.','Era 5 and the other current Headquarters objectives are complete.'],next:'At MT9, build the XP economy and divisor reductions needed for a much longer Era push. Faster Era software helps only after the tower can handle the enemies it reaches.',sources:['S01','S02','S08']
},
{
tier:9,title:'Make Era divisors affordable',phase:'Era progression',
goal:'Develop an XP-and-divisor loop that can carry you toward Era 1,000.',
terms:[['XP','The in-run currency used for temporary improvements. It is different from permanent Workshop resources.'],['Era creeping','A community strategy of advancing gradually, earning XP, upgrading divisors and only then moving farther.'],['/Inf','A divisor display associated with an effectively infinite numerical value. Older routes aim for it; its usefulness and costs depend on current game mechanics.']],
targets:['Work toward the documented Era 1,000 milestone. Check Headquarters for the current collection and total-tier requirements.','Improve the affordability of the in-run divisors before relying on rapid Era skipping.'],
steps:[['Develop the research leading to experiments','Continue Era Workshop progress until the relevant experiment upgrades are available. Focus on reductions affecting the XP price of your in-run divisors. The community references flags older cost targets as obsolete, so compare current prices rather than chasing a number from an old screenshot.',['S08','S01']],['Build an XP run that stays alive','Use a blueprint able to kill enemies steadily without dying or advancing past its own capability. If acceleration jumps too far ahead, temporarily disable the software causing that behavior while you establish the run’s upgrades. This is a controlled farming phase, not a permanent rejection of acceleration.',['S08']],['Buy both sides of the solution','Invest in the relevant damage and health divisors rather than assuming one solves everything. Spend XP, advance enough to earn more, and repeat while watching survival. If an elemental power is the actual cause of failure, address its disable as well.',['S08']],['Use acceleration after the run is prepared','Once the divisor loop works reliably, enable suitable Era progression software such as Era Surge. Observe the first jump: if the tower immediately fails, return to the setup phase and identify the missing counter or divisor.',['S07','S08']],['Choose perks around the limiting resource','The community references recommends considering International Experts for Town Perk scaling, and Combat Surveillance where XP income is the limiting factor. Read their current effects and compare them with what your run lacks; neither is a substitute for completing the necessary Era research.',['S05']]],
trouble:[['An old guide says I need exactly 355 XP per upgrade','Do not treat that historical target as a current requirement. Costs have changed; use your game’s actual researched prices.'],['I survive low Eras but die after enabling all software','Acceleration may be outrunning your upgrades. Build the divisors and disables first, then test progression software in a controlled way.']],
check:['Your run can fund its needed divisors before advancing beyond its strength.','Era 1,000 and all current promotion objectives are complete.'],next:'MT10 adds a special discovery to the Era-depth goal. Reaching a large Era number alone will not necessarily satisfy that discovery.',sources:['S01','S02','S05','S07','S08']
},
{
tier:10,title:'Make the Neutral Stone discovery',phase:'Era progression',
goal:'Reach Era 10 million and complete the separate discovery associated with the Neutral Infinity Stone.',
terms:[['Neutral Era disable','The action that disables Neutral’s Era power during a tower test. It has a special role in the discovery at this stage.'],['Discovery versus unlocking','Finding a stone and completing its later unlock conditions are different steps. This guide focuses on the discovery needed for promotion.']],
targets:['Work toward the documented Era 10,000,000 goal.','Complete the special discovery: the community references describes disabling Neutral’s Era power at Era 1,000,000 or later. Check that Headquarters actually registers it.'],
steps:[['Stabilize your existing Era setup','Use the divisor and XP process from MT9 until your runs reliably reach the necessary upgrades. Check that the elements in your chosen region do not introduce an unhandled power. A discovery attempt is easier when the underlying run is dependable.',['S08']],['Research the available acceleration','Inspect Era Surge and Era Burst and improve available research. Experiment rewards may supply useful Era modules; evaluate them in the context of your actual blueprint rather than automatically replacing everything.',['S07','S08']],['Plan the discovery before the counter races past it','Make sure you understand where the Neutral power control is and how to pay its disable cost. Use a run where you can perform the required action at the relevant Era. Slow or pause the progression process as needed to inspect the condition instead of assuming a fast highscore run will complete it automatically.',['S02','S09']],['Perform the action and check the objective','At Era one million or later, disable Neutral as described in the supplied source. Inspect the discovery or Headquarters objective afterward. If it did not register, read the current condition carefully before spending time on a new full-length push.',['S02','S09']],['Finish depth and collection requirements','Continue to Era ten million, then complete any remaining module objectives. A highscore and a discovery are separate achievements; confirm both before expecting promotion.',['S02']]],
trouble:[['I reached ten million but promotion is still blocked','Inspect the special-discovery line. A large Era record does not prove you performed the Neutral action.'],['Do I have to repeat the whole climb?','The community references describes revisiting a sufficiently high record with Critical Wavejump. Check your current software behavior and the discovery condition before attempting that recovery route.']],
check:['Headquarters confirms the special discovery.','The Era and module objectives are all complete.'],next:'MT11’s next challenge is the scale of the journey. Build acceleration across the map before trying to brute-force the route to the first Infinity.',sources:['S02','S07','S08','S09']
},
{
tier:11,title:'Prepare the journey to Infinity',phase:'Era progression',
goal:'Use broader Era records and acceleration to reach Era 100 billion, the documented boundary for Infinity 1.',
terms:[['Region/difficulty record','A highscore for one specific region and difficulty. Different difficulties in one region create separate records.'],['Wave Horizon','Software whose benefits depend on broader Era progress. The supplied strategy develops Era records across multiple region/difficulty combinations.'],['Infinity 1','The next progression scale reached after the late-Era milestone. Its combat changes are introduced in the MT12 guide.']],
targets:['Work toward Era 100,000,000,000 / the first Infinity milestone and confirm the current MT12 checklist.','Improve record coverage and acceleration while keeping the established Era combat solution intact.'],
steps:[['Secure the Era combat foundation','Before a long push, verify that your divisors and disables work on the chosen region. Speed software cannot compensate for a blueprint that stops killing or dies when the run reaches its next elemental obstacle.',['S08']],['Create Era records on more combinations','Reach Era 1 on additional regions and difficulties that you can handle. The older community route aims toward 90 combinations, from 15 regions times six difficulties. Treat this as an acceleration project rather than a statement that all 90 are mandatory to promote.',['S07','S10','S12']],['Use Wave Horizon with the other unlocked tools','Review Wave Horizon, Wave Marathon and your available Era software. Make sure the relevant research is complete, then inspect how these tools interact with your records. The value of broader coverage is that it improves the progress of future runs, not just the score on the current one.',['S07','S10']],['Separate preparation from the final push','Older community strategies build acceleration before enabling the strongest Era-skipping tools. Try this only with an understood software setup and watch the effect. Do not treat a historical time or acceleration target as a verified current requirement.',['S10','S12']],['Register Infinity and finish the promotion','Once the milestone is reached, check Headquarters rather than assuming the game has completed all module requirements for you. After MT12, inspect the newly unlocked Infinity Workshop before pushing farther with the old blueprint.',['S02','S09']]],
trouble:[['The tower is safe but the ETA is enormous','Review software, research and cross-map record coverage. This stage often has a progression-speed bottleneck rather than a damage bottleneck.'],['An imported old blueprint or timer does not work','Check module availability, upgraded tiers and patch changes. The linked older routes provide ideas, not guarantees for a current save.']],
check:['The first Infinity milestone is recorded.','All other MT12 objectives are complete.'],next:'Infinity enemies introduce a different combat problem. Learn the new modules and fragment systems before assuming an Era blueprint can continue unchanged.',sources:['S02','S07','S08','S09','S10','S12']
},
{
tier:12,title:'Build your first Infinity-capable tower',phase:'Infinity progression',
goal:'Learn Infinity combat, reach Infinity 2, and establish Infinity progress in three different regions.',
terms:[['Module fragments','A reward used for Infinity development. Check the Infinity Workshop to see what each purchase consumes.'],['Infinity Grid','A progression board for unlocking additional Infinity modules. Plan a path toward something your current build needs.'],['Infinity Anvil','A system for purchasing supporting bonuses. Choose bonuses around the specific problem holding back your run.'],['Module copying','Infinity enemies can imitate equipped modules. Adding a strong module may therefore change the threat you face as well as your own strength.']],
targets:['The supplied tier reference describes Infinity 2 and Infinity reached in three distinct regions. Confirm the current objective text.','Meet the current module and total-tier thresholds without using the outdated historical totals.'],
steps:[['Inspect your first Infinity module rewards','After your first Infinity, look for Infinity Attack and Infinity Foundation and learn their effects. They provide the starting offense and survival tools described in the Infinity reference. Upgrade and equip the tools needed for the new enemies instead of carrying the old Era setup forward without review.',['S09']],['Run a small, understandable Infinity build','Test one region and watch what ends the run. If you survive but cannot kill, improve the relevant Infinity offense; if you die immediately, inspect foundation, defenses and copied enemy effects. Change a small number of modules so you can identify which change helped.',['S09']],['Spend fragments toward a useful Grid route','Review available unlock paths and choose a module that addresses your present problem. A planned route avoids scattering fragments over unrelated unlocks while a missing core tool continues to block progress.',['S09']],['Support the build through the Anvil','Choose damage, resistance, XP or resource bonuses according to your actual limit. Spend for a measurable improvement, test again, and then choose the next purchase. The right balance can differ between a farming run and a depth push.',['S09']],['Track depth and geography separately','Push to Infinity 2 on a suitable region, then reach Infinity on three different regions. Three difficulties of Forest still represent one region for the regional requirement. Keep a small region checklist so the distinction is visible.',['S02','S10']]],
trouble:[['My old Era build stopped working','Infinity enemies resist ordinary approaches and can copy modules. Rebuild around the Infinity mechanics instead of adding more of the same old damage.'],['I have three Infinity scores but the region counter says one','Check whether all three scores belong to different difficulties of the same region.']],
check:['The required Infinity depth is recorded.','The regional counter shows three distinct regions, or the current number Headquarters requests.','The module objectives are complete.'],next:'After reaching MT13, inspect Wave Breach and Era Tunneling to improve the repeated trips needed for broader regional progress.',sources:['S02','S09','S10']
},
{
tier:13,title:'Expand Infinity across the map',phase:'Infinity progression',
goal:'Reach Infinity 5 and build Infinity records in ten distinct regions.',
terms:[['Depth push','A run aimed at a higher Infinity number in a favorable region.'],['Coverage run','A run aimed at recording Infinity in another region. It does not have to be your deepest run to advance the regional objective.'],['Eternity Anvil','A military perk related to Anvil purchasing area. The supplied community route recommends considering it when the Anvil is a major investment.']],
targets:['The community references describes Infinity 5 plus Infinity reached in ten different regions for MT14. Confirm those and the current module thresholds in Headquarters.','Maintain separate plans for deeper Infinity and broader map coverage.'],
steps:[['Keep a reliable baseline blueprint','Start from the Infinity build that worked at MT12. When another region fails, inspect its enemies and copied effects before making wholesale changes. A targeted adjustment is easier to understand than replacing every module at once.',['S09']],['Use the new travel-speed tools','Research and inspect Wave Breach and Era Tunneling where available. The community references warns that Era Tunneling changed in v1.0, so follow the current tooltip rather than an old timing recipe.',['S07','S01']],['Complete region coverage methodically','Choose a new region, reach the required Infinity record, and mark it complete. Favor manageable difficulties while satisfying the regional objective. Raising additional difficulties can support other systems, but it does not add another distinct region.',['S02','S09']],['Push depth where the build performs best','Use your most reliable region to work toward Infinity 5. Compare failed attempts: inability to kill, copied defenses and inability to survive call for different module or Anvil changes. Use fragments to address the observed problem.',['S09']],['Choose long-term support deliberately','Consider Eternity Anvil if purchasing reach is limiting Anvil development, or compare other available perks such as Twin Singularity against your goals. Perk advice in community routes is a preference based on a strategy, not a mandatory promotion condition.',['S05','S12']]],
trouble:[['One region keeps failing while others are easy','Inspect the regional elements and copied module interactions. A successful blueprint in one environment is not proof that every region is equivalent.'],['I am improving the wrong counter','A deeper Infinity in an already-counted region helps depth, but it does not turn nine regions into ten.']],
check:['The required depth and ten-region coverage are recorded.','Current module objectives are complete.'],next:'MT14 combines broader Infinity coverage with the Infinity Stone requirements. Start building-specific tasks early so they do not become the final wait.',sources:['S01','S02','S05','S07','S09','S12']
},
{
tier:14,title:'Complete the map and unlock the stones',phase:'Infinity progression',
goal:'Work toward Infinity 25, Infinity in all 15 regions, and the stone-related discovery needed for MT15.',
terms:[['Collected stone','A stone you have found. Collection alone is not the same as completing its unlock requirements.'],['Infinity Board','The place to inspect your stone progress and the conditions still missing. Use its current wording when older guides disagree.'],['90-combination coverage','Infinity progress across 15 regions and six difficulties. This is a broader acceleration project than the 15-distinct-region promotion objective.']],
targets:['The community references describes Infinity 25, all 15 regions and Dr. Cubical’s discovery, associated with unlocking all ten stones. Confirm the current Headquarters wording.','Use the Infinity Board to track each stone’s collection, building task and shared requirements separately.'],
steps:[['Finish the missing regions first','Reach Infinity on each still-uncompleted region with a reliable blueprint. Record the regional objective separately from progress on additional difficulties. Improving 90 combinations may help later acceleration, but do not confuse it with the basic 15-region checklist.',['S02','S11']],['Choose a favorable region for Infinity 25','Use your strongest sustainable Infinity build and support it through relevant Grid and Anvil purchases. Diagnose failed pushes before spending: if the problem is survival, more acceleration can simply reach the failure faster.',['S09']],['Collect and unlock stones deliberately','Consult the stone planning table below, then read the actual Infinity Board requirements. The community references describes non-Neutral collection through appropriately disabled enemies of the associated element at Infinity or beyond. Check the exact condition for each stone rather than assuming visiting its region is sufficient.',['S09']],['Work on building conditions in parallel','Shipments, Factory construction and Laboratory progression can take time. Start the conditions you can satisfy while improving the remaining regional records. Revisit the Board after each milestone to confirm that it counts.',['S09']],['Complete the final promotion checklist','Confirm depth, region count, module objectives and discovery independently in Headquarters. A collected stone, an unlocked stone and a charged stone are different states; do not substitute one for another when checking a requirement.',['S02','S09']]],
trouble:[['I own every stone but the discovery is incomplete','Inspect unlock conditions on the Board. Owning the icons is not proof that the building and shared requirements are complete.'],['Two guides give different essence requirements','Use the current Board. The community references explicitly flags disagreement between older sources.']],
check:['The required Infinity depth and all distinct regions are recorded.','Each stone has the state requested by the discovery objective.','Headquarters confirms every MT15 objective.'],next:'MT15 opens the final-boss stage. Stone activation and charging still matter; the promotion itself is not the entire preparation for the fight.',sources:['S02','S09','S11'],stoneTable:true
},
{
tier:15,title:'Prepare for the final boss and endgame',phase:'Infinity progression',
goal:'Use your completed military progression to prepare for Thorus and improve the systems supporting repeated endgame runs.',
terms:[['Stone charging','The process that prepares Infinity Stones for their effects. Finding and unlocking them does not mean charging is finished.'],['Infinity Power','A benefit associated with regional Infinity records that supports stone charging, according to the supplied Infinity reference.'],['Exotic Refinements','Permanent improvements that can support endgame charging. Compare their current costs and effects before spending.']],
targets:['There is no MT16 route in this MT0–15 guide. Your next objective is the final-boss preparation and whatever endgame goals you choose.','Inspect the current final-boss entry conditions and relevant insurance upgrades before committing resources.'],
steps:[['Read the boss-entry condition','The community references identifies Thorus as the final boss and describes activating all Infinity Stones through the special toggle perk near the bottom of the Infinity perk list for the first fight. Confirm the current control and prerequisites in your game; reaching MT15 alone is not the entire entry process.',['S02','S09']],['Finish charging preparation','Review which stone is slowing the setup and what currently determines its charging time. Keep collection, unlocking, activation and charging as separate checklist items so you do not repeatedly solve a condition already complete.',['S09']],['Improve regional Infinity records','Continue building stronger records where your blueprint can progress efficiently. The supplied source connects these records to Infinity Power and faster charging. Favor improvements you can obtain reliably before forcing an extremely expensive push for a small benefit.',['S09']],['Compare permanent charging investments','Review Exotic Refinements and the available duration reductions. The supplied Infinity guidance favors considering duration reductions once basic upgrades are established. Evaluate the benefit against your actual charging wait instead of assuming every linear bonus deserves another purchase.',['S09']],['Prepare resources and learn from attempts','Read the relevant insurance upgrades and fight controls. After an attempt, identify the specific mechanic or resource that limited it and prepare that part before retrying. This page provides endgame preparation, not an independently tested boss combat script or a guarantee of a first-attempt victory.',['S09']]],
trouble:[['I reached MT15 but cannot start the fight','Check the stone activation and entry conditions rather than looking for another military promotion.'],['Most of my time is spent waiting for stones','Compare regional record improvements and permanent charging upgrades. Diagnose the charging bottleneck separately from tower combat strength.']],
check:['The current boss-entry conditions are understood and satisfied.','You have checked stone states, charging and the relevant resource costs.'],next:'Return to earlier tier guides for the systems you want to improve, or use the source library for more specialized routes. Older imported blueprints and scripts still need checking against your version.',sources:['S02','S09']
}
];
